{
  "rules": [
    "Near-term (≈1–10d) forecasts are slightly low on average; apply a small upward bias correction (~+0.2 to +0.6 €/t) unless the market is already accelerating strongly.",
    "Mid-horizon (≈10–22d) the model tends to be too high during drawdowns (predicts a slower drop than realized); increase downside sensitivity by subtracting ~0.5–1.5 €/t when recent returns turn negative.",
    "Late-horizon (≈23–30d) the model systematically underestimates upside breakouts after consolidation; add a ramp-up premium that grows with horizon (e.g., +1 €/t at day 23 rising to +5–9 €/t by day 30) when the last 1–2 weeks show higher highs / rising trend.",
    "The model exhibits lag to regime shifts: after a trend reversal, it stays anchored to the prior level. Use a momentum/regime filter: if 5–10d momentum is positive, shift the whole path upward; if negative, shift downward (stronger adjustment for negative momentum in days 10–22).",
    "After sharp upward spikes, the model mean-reverts too quickly (caps prices in the high-60s) while realized prices can continue trending higher; reduce mean-reversion strength and allow higher terminal levels when volatility + momentum are elevated."
  ]
}
